---
id: org.dxos.review.ui
name: Ui
version: 0.1.0
---

UI authoring rules distilled from the `composer-ui`, `reactivity`, and
`composite-components` skills — the design-system and reactivity conventions a
linter cannot express. Each `rule` block below is discovered by the
agentic-review harness, matched against the files its globs select, and applied
by a focused subagent.

## Extensions

| Term   | URI                   |
| ------ | --------------------- |
| `rule` | org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0 |

## Rules

```mdl
rule no-dead-logical-utilities: The `tailwindcss-logical` classes compile to nothing — use the live forms
  The `tailwindcss-logical` dialect was dropped in the Tailwind v4 migration, so
  every class it provided now compiles to NOTHING, silently: nothing errors,
  nothing lints, the layout is merely wrong — and when the dead class was
  load-bearing (a `min-bs-*` floor reserving height, a `min-is-0` letting a grid
  child shrink) the failure surfaces far from its cause. Flag the dead infixes
  `-is-` / `-bs-` / `-li-` / `-lb-`: `pis-*`/`pie-*` → `ps-*`/`pe-*`,
  `pbs-*`/`pbe-*` → `pt-*`/`pb-*`, `pli-*`/`plb-*` → `px-*`/`py-*`,
  `mis-*`/`mie-*` → `ms-*`/`me-*`, `mbs-*`/`mbe-*` → `mt-*`/`mb-*`,
  `mli-*`/`mlb-*` → `mx-*`/`my-*`, `is-*`/`bs-*` → `w-*`/`h-*`, and their
  `min-`/`max-` forms. Sizing is physical (`w-*`, `h-*`, `size-*`); direction-
  sensitive spacing stays logical. Tailwind's own logical spacing — `ps-*`/`pe-*`,
  `ms-*`/`me-*`, `start-*`/`end-*`, `border-s`/`border-e`,
  `text-start`/`text-end` — is NOT a violation and must not be rewritten to
  physical (`pl-`, `ml-`, `left-`, `text-left`).
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.tsx
    - packages/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/*.css
  grep: \b(p|m)(is|ie|bs|be|li|lb)-|\b(min-|max-)?(is|bs)-
  severity: error
```

```mdl
rule themed-primitives-take-classNames: Style a themed primitive through `classNames`, never `className`
  Every `@dxos/react-ui*` primitive exposes `classNames` (string or array) and
  reconciles it with the theme's own output through `tx()`; a `className` passed
  to one is either dropped or silently overrides the themed value instead of
  merging with it. The same rule holds when authoring a primitive: the
  consumer-facing prop is `classNames` and the part converts it internally —
  never publish `className` on a composite part's props. Flag a `className` prop
  on a capitalized component imported from a `@dxos/react-ui*` package, and a
  composite part whose exported Props type surfaces `className`. `className` on
  an intrinsic element (`<div>`, `<span>`, `<svg>`) is NOT a violation, and
  neither is the internal `className` a part hands to `ark.*` or to a wrapped
  Ark/Radix primitive.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.tsx
  grep: className=
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-invented-theme-tokens: Color classes come from the theme's custom properties, never from a guess
  Color tokens are plain Tailwind classes generated from `--color-<name>` custom
  properties in `packages/ui/ui-theme/src/css/theme/semantic.css`, so
  `--color-modal-surface` yields `bg-modal-surface` / `text-modal-surface` /
  `border-modal-surface` and nothing else does. An invented token (`bg-input`,
  `text-primary`, `border-muted`) is not in the theme, produces no CSS, and
  renders wrong in exactly one of the two themes — white-on-white in dark mode —
  which is invisible until someone toggles it. Flag a `bg-`/`text-`/`border-`
  class whose name is neither a Tailwind palette value nor a `--color-` property
  declared in `semantic.css`; the live families are the surfaces
  (`bg-base-surface`, `bg-card-surface`, `bg-modal-surface`,
  `bg-toolbar-surface`, `bg-sidebar-surface`, `bg-deck-surface`,
  `bg-group-surface`, `bg-input-surface`, `bg-hover-surface`,
  `bg-attention-surface`, `bg-accent-bg`), the text tokens (`text-base-fg`,
  `text-description`, `text-subdued`, `text-placeholder`, `text-accent-text`)
  and the separators (`border-separator`, `border-subdued-separator`,
  `border-primary-separator`, `border-active-separator`, `border-focus-ring`).
  Functional layout hints (`p-4`, `space-y-4`, `@container`) are NOT violations.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.tsx
  grep: (bg|text|border)-[a-z][a-z-]+
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-styling-wrapper-divs: Boxes come from Flex/Grid/Column/Container, not a hand-rolled `<div className='flex …'>`
  A wrapper `<div>` added for styling breaks the height chain `ScrollArea`
  depends on — a wrapper around an input once silently disabled scrolling — and
  a hand-rolled flex or grid div is new debt in a census someone is actively
  driving down (`packages/ui/react-ui/AUDIT.md`: 145 of 191 converted). Use
  `Flex` (`column`, `gap`, `align`, `justify`, `grow`, `center`), `Grid`
  (`cols`, `rows`, `gap`, including the list form `cols={['min-content','1fr']}`
  and `cols='subgrid'`), `Column` for the gutter grid, and `Container` to merely
  fill the parent; all take `asChild`, so the layout projects onto a semantic
  element at no extra DOM node. `gap` takes ramp steps (`xs | sm | md | lg | xl |
  2xl | form | form-section`), never a Tailwind number. Prefer `asChild` on a
  `Panel.*` part whose single child is itself composable rather than nesting a
  box inside it. Flag a `<div>` whose classes include `flex`/`grid` or which
  exists only to carry padding or spacing, and a `gap-<number>` literal on a
  layout primitive. A box with genuinely ad-hoc positioning that no primitive
  expresses is NOT a violation, but say why in a comment.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/ui/**/*.tsx
  grep: <div[^>]*className=['"`][^'"`]*(flex|grid)
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-hand-rolled-lists: Any collection of rows is a `@dxos/react-ui-list` primitive
  A `map()` over `<div>`/`<li>` rows re-implements keyboard navigation,
  current-item and selection state, and the theme's row rhythm — badly, and
  differently in every plugin. `Listbox` (headless; omit `value`/`onValueChange`
  for a non-selectable `role=list`) covers flat display and single-select lists,
  `OrderedList` covers reorderable / master-detail, `Tree` / `Accordion` cover
  hierarchy, and the Mosaic `Stack` (`@dxos/react-ui-mosaic`) covers reorderable
  or resizable collections of surfaces; check for an existing `Picker` /
  `Combobox` / `SearchList` before building any chooser. `dx-current` and
  `dx-selected` are driven by the primitives — do not set them or wire focus by
  hand — and the primitives own their padding, so they drop straight into a
  `ScrollArea.Viewport` with no padded wrapper. Flag a mapped stack of row
  elements, any import of the deprecated `@dxos/react-ui-stack`, and any use of
  `@dxos/react-ui`'s deprecated core `List` / `ListItem`. A `map()` producing
  non-row content (grid cells, chips, chart marks, `Listbox.Item` children) is
  NOT a violation.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/ui/**/*.tsx
  grep: \.map\(|react-ui-stack|ListItem
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-native-form-controls: Edit objects with the schema-driven `Form`, never a native input
  A native `<input>` / `<textarea>` / `<select>` in a plugin inherits none of the
  theme — a bare textarea is a white box in dark mode — and bypasses schema
  validation entirely. Render editors from the Effect Schema with `Form` from
  `@dxos/react-ui-form`, composed as `Form.Root` (renders no DOM) + `Viewport` /
  `Content` / `FieldSet`. The form never mutates `values`: the parent applies the
  change. A field the schema cannot express takes a custom renderer built from
  themed primitives (`Input.*`, `Select.*`), registered statically per field or
  through `fieldProvider` when the set is only known at runtime — never a native
  element. Flag a native form element in plugin or UI code and a form component
  that writes into its own `values`. Native elements inside the `react-ui-form` /
  `react-ui` primitives that implement the themed controls are NOT violations.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
  grep: <(input|textarea|select)\b
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule toolbars-are-menu-actions: Container toolbars are built from menu actions with an `attendableId`
  A bare chain of `Toolbar.IconButton`s is closed to plugin composition: graph
  actions, plugin extensions, and keyboard shortcuts all arrive as menu actions,
  so a toolbar that does not compose them silently drops half the app's
  behaviour. Build actions with `MenuBuilder` inside an `Atom`, thread them
  through `useMenuActions`, render with `Menu.Root`, and always pass the
  `attendableId` from `AppSurface.ObjectArticleProps` so attention-driven
  contributions target the right surface — never underscore it as unused. Encode
  dynamic state (disabled, busy/idle icon swap) in the action properties and list
  that state in the `useMemo` deps, so the atom rebuilds and the toolbar updates.
  Flag a container rendering `Toolbar.IconButton` children directly, and a
  toolbar built without `attendableId`. A toolbar inside a low-level `react-ui-*`
  component that takes its actions as props is NOT a violation.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
  grep: Toolbar\.|useMenuActions|attendableId
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule subscribe-where-you-read: Reading an ECHO object or a ref in render requires a subscription
  An ECHO object is a singleton with stable identity, so a bare property read in
  render (`<h1>{doc.title}</h1>`) renders once and never updates — no prop ever
  changes. It compiles, and it survives manual testing; only navigation or a
  concurrent edit exposes it, as data that appears after navigating away and
  back. Subscribe where you read, as narrowly as you read: `useObject(obj)` for
  the object, `useObject(obj, 'field')` when the component renders one field (a
  whole-object subscription re-renders on every unrelated change), `useQuery` for
  a collection, and a single derived atom (`Atom.make((get) => …)` over
  `Obj.atom` / `Obj.atomProperty`) instead of a pileup of hooks plus a `useMemo`
  when the value combines several sources. `ref.target` in render is the same
  defect with a worse symptom: it is `undefined` until the target loads and never
  notifies when it arrives, so the component wedges empty — use `useObject(ref)`,
  or `useResolveRef(ref)` when a handler needs the live object. Flag a property
  access on an ECHO object or a `.target` read in a render path with no backing
  subscription; a surface receiving `AppSurface.ObjectArticleProps<T>` must
  subscribe at the point it reads fields. `.target` inside a callback that runs
  after the subscription established the object is loaded is NOT a violation, and
  neither are purely presentational components that receive scalars.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/ui/**/*.tsx
  grep: \.target\b|useObject|ObjectArticleProps
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule leaf-owns-its-subscription: A list subscribes to membership only; each tile subscribes to its own item
  A list that resolves its refs — `refs.map((ref) => ref.target)`, or
  `useObjects(refs)` — has two defects: cold loads drop items or need a
  re-render-trigger hack, and the list now subscribes to every element, so one
  item's edit re-renders every tile. The list owns exactly ONE subscription, the
  membership (the ref-array property, or the query), and re-renders on
  add/remove/reorder and nothing else; the refs pass through unresolved and each
  leaf owns the subscription to its own item, rendering a fallback until it
  loads. Prefer a scoped `useQuery` (`Filter.childOf(container)`) over walking a
  ref array when membership is queryable — query subscriptions fire on
  membership and order only — and apply container-defined order as a client-side
  sort. Flag a `.target` or `useObject` call inside a list's `map()`, and any new
  `useObjects` call site — it is deprecated for exactly this reason. Load-only
  ref resolution at the list level is a tolerated stopgap when a child's
  structure derives from target fields; list-level context (column templates,
  shared handlers) reaching a tile through props or context is NOT a violation as
  long as the ECHO data still enters at the leaf.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/ui/**/*.tsx
  grep: useObjects|\.map\(.*\.target|\.map\(
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule write-through-the-live-object: Writes target the live object or the atom setter, never a snapshot
  Subscriptions hand back snapshots — immutable values for reading — so assigning
  to one is a silent no-op, or a type error hidden behind a cast. Write through
  the live object: `Obj.update(subject, (obj) => …)`, or the update callback
  `useObject` returns; append inside that callback with `push`, and reach for
  `as Obj.Mutable<T>` inside `Obj.update` when the intentionally narrow snapshot
  type gets in the way. The same rule holds for the other two stores: an effect
  that mirrors an ECHO field into `useState`, or copies one atom into another,
  creates a second source of truth that goes stale on the next remote edit —
  derive, don't sync. And shared state travels as a single (writable) atom prop,
  not a `value` + `onChange` pair threaded through layers that then each couple to
  its shape; `value`/`onChange` stays correct for a leaf control wrapping a DOM
  input. Flag an assignment to a snapshot, a `useEffect` whose only job is to
  copy between stores, and a `value` + `onChange` pair drilled past one layer.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/ui/**/*.tsx
  grep: useObject|Obj\.update|onChange
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule structural-regions-use-design-system-components: Dialog and card headers come from design-system parts, never ad hoc divs
  This is the remaining slice of "structural regions come from the design
  system" not already covered: `no-styling-wrapper-divs` covers generic
  layout wrappers, `no-native-form-controls` covers form fields, and
  `toolbars-are-menu-actions` covers toolbars. Left uncovered are dialog/card
  headers built from styled divs instead of their own parts, and a
  "primitive" component whose own consumers must wrap it for ordinary
  layout — the primitive should own its layout instead. Flag a `<div>`/`<span>`
  tree styled with utility classes to look like a dialog body or card header
  instead of `Dialog.Header`/`Dialog.Body`/`Card.Header`; a primitive
  component whose consumers add a wrapper div around it for spacing or
  alignment the primitive should provide itself. Do not flag genuinely ad hoc
  positioning no primitive expresses, when the diff says why, or a case
  already covered by the three rules named above.
  Source: 8 review comments across 6 PRs, e.g. https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/9964#discussion_r2414936444.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/ui/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule story-for-new-ui-component: A new container or component ships with a Storybook story at the right level
  A component with no story has no resting-state snapshot to catch a visual
  regression, and a story pitched at the wrong level either drags in
  dependencies (client, database, plugin manager) a plain component never
  touches, or tests a child instead of the container actually under review.
  Flag a newly added container/component `.tsx` file with no matching
  `.stories.tsx` alongside it; a story that renders a lower-level child
  instead of the container under review, or wires up dependencies the
  component itself never uses. Do not flag a component with no meaningful
  visual state to snapshot (a pure logic hook, a non-visual utility), or an
  existing component that is merely modified, not newly added. Fix: add the
  story at the correct component level with a resting-state screenshot.
  Source: 5 review comments across 4 PRs, e.g. https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10719#discussion_r2923831311.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/ui/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  context: diff, siblings
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule extract-non-rendering-logic-from-component: Move derived-state and lifecycle logic out of the component body into a hook or function
  An inline `useMemo`/`useEffect` building a filter/query, or managing an
  open-on-mount/close-on-unmount resource lifecycle directly in a component
  body, hides logic that has nothing to do with rendering inside the
  component that's supposed to just render — and a hook whose body is mostly
  `if (!x) return` guard staircases is really several smaller, well-typed
  pieces stacked into one. Flag an inline `useMemo`/`useEffect` building a
  filter/query or managing a resource's lifecycle directly in a component; a
  hook whose body is mostly non-null guard clauses on its own inputs. Do not
  flag a small, genuinely one-off derivation with no reuse potential and
  nothing worth hiding. Fix: extract a named hook (e.g. a generic
  `useResource(get, deps)`) or a well-typed helper function.
  Source: 4 review comments across 4 PRs, e.g. https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10024#discussion_r2439823314.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/ui/**/*.tsx
  grep: useEffect\(|useMemo\(
  unit: file
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule design-tokens-not-raw-spacing-sizing: Spacing and sizing come from the design system's tokens, not raw Tailwind or arbitrary values
  This is the spacing/sizing half of using semantic tokens; the color half is
  `no-invented-theme-tokens`. A bracketed arbitrary value (`w-[12rem]`) or a
  raw Tailwind spacing/sizing class where a semantic token exists (`is-48`)
  bypasses the design system, and two visually related components drifting
  onto different sizing tokens for the same role with no stated reason erodes
  the exact consistency tokens exist to keep. Flag a raw bracketed arbitrary
  value or Tailwind spacing/sizing class where a semantic token exists; a
  sibling component using a different sizing token for the same visual role
  with no explanation. Do not flag functional layout utilities (`p-4`,
  `space-y-4`, `@container`) that aren't token questions. Fix: swap in the
  matching semantic token and align sibling components on one token.
  Source: 6 review comments across 3 PRs, e.g. https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/9891#discussion_r2380558993.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/ui/**/*.tsx
  grep: \[[0-9]
  unit: file
  context: siblings
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-wrapper-div-around-asChild-single-child: A composite's asChild/single-child slot takes the actionable element directly, never a wrapper div
  A Radix/Ark `.Trigger` using `asChild`, or a structural slot like
  `StackItem.Root`'s `Surface` child, requires exactly one immediate
  actionable or structural element; a styled `<div>` interposed between them
  breaks that single-child contract at runtime, sometimes silently. Flag a
  `<div>` between `asChild` (or an equivalent structural slot) and its target
  component; a sub-part rendered outside its own `Root`; `asChild` given more
  than one child element. Do not flag a wrapper div that changes semantics
  deliberately and is not participating in `asChild`/slot composition. Fix:
  remove the wrapper and pass the actionable component directly as the single
  child.
  Source: 4 review comments across 2 PRs, e.g. https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10012#discussion_r2433349336.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/ui/**/*.tsx
  grep: asChild
  unit: file
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule reactive-state-via-atom-bridge: Reactively-observed state is backed by an atom through the established bridge, not a hand-rolled subscription
  A custom `useEffect`+`useState` hook that reimplements what the
  Stream→Atom→`useAtom` bridge already does, or a getter that returns a
  `Promise`/one-shot `Effect` for state the UI actually needs to watch over
  time, quietly falls out of sync with the rest of the app's reactivity —
  and a custom reactive runtime threaded through React context defeats the
  point of a context that should only ever provide services. Flag a custom
  subscription hook reimplementing the Stream→Atom bridge; a getter returning
  a `Promise`/one-shot `Effect` for state the UI needs to watch; a custom
  reactive runtime threaded through context. Do not flag state that is
  genuinely one-shot — fetched once, never expected to change under the
  component. Fix: back the value with an `Atom` and consume it via the
  standard bridge/`useAtom`.
  Source: 3 review comments across 2 PRs, e.g. https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/12185#discussion_r3587540903.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.ts
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/ui/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule layout-only-wrapper-invisible-to-a11y: A wrapper that exists only for layout renders as a Fragment or is marked invisible to assistive tech
  A wrapper added purely to group children still shows up as a meaningful
  node in the accessibility tree unless it renders as a `Fragment` or is
  explicitly marked out of it, which means a screen reader announces
  structure that carries no actual meaning. Flag a wrapper `<div>` added only
  to group children, with no `role='none'` and no `Fragment` alternative
  considered. Do not flag a wrapper that also carries styling or semantics
  beyond pure grouping. Fix: replace with `<>...</>` where no DOM node is
  needed, or add `role='none'` where one is.
  Source: 2 review comments across 2 PRs, e.g. https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10251#discussion_r2585340352.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/ui/**/*.tsx
  grep: <div
  unit: file
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

## Appendix

```mdl
ext rule
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0
  desc: A machine-checked review rule applied by an LLM subagent or the System One checker.
  inline-prose: true
  fields:
    files:       Glob | Glob[]     # paths this rule reviews, relative to `scope`
    grep?:       Prose             # optional regex pre-filter over file contents
    severity?:   warn | error      # defaults to warn
    scope?:      dir | repo        # glob base: this file's dir (default) or repo root
    unit?:       file | pr         # one verdict per file (default) or per change set
    context?:    ContextKind[]     # diff | imports | importers | siblings | package | public-api | similar | test | pr
    system-one?: on | off          # off when no fetcher can supply what the rule needs
    question?:   Prose             # literal yes/no question for the System One checker
```
